<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Fraga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1134">1134</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a> was fought in July 1134 when an Almoravid relief army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a>, who was besieging the town of Fraga on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinca">Cinca</a>. Alfonso escaped but died a few weeks later, leaving his kingdom by will to the military orders. The will was set aside, and the crisis led to the separation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from Aragon and the recall of his brother Ramiro II from the cloister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fraga.jpg" alt="Battle of Fraga" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Pradilla y Ortiz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonso_I_de_Arag%C3%B3n_por_Pradilla_(1879).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From the second half of the 11th century the kings of Aragon and the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and Urgel sought again and again to conquer the towns and frontier fortresses of the Marca Superior held by the Muslims. Their aim was the lowland around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segre">Segre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinca">Cinca</a> rivers down to the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, a prosperous region with direct access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>, whose chief towns were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a>, Mequinenza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a>.</p><p>Alfonso the Battler, known to the Muslims as Ibn Rudmir or al-Farandji, laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a> in July 1134 with an army from Aragon, fighting alongside Viscount Centule VI of Béarn. The Almoravid response was swift: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a>, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, equipped 2,000 horsemen, the Emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> 500, and the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> another 200, and the combined force marched to relieve the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The relief army, led by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lérida</a>, Ibn ‘Iyad, arrived on 17 July 1134. Seeing it, the defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a> sallied out, while Alfonso, still confident of his numbers and his tactics, rallied his troops. His entourage clashed with the cavalry of the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ghaniya">Yahya ibn Ghaniya</a>, which cut down many of the Aragonese. The townspeople then fell on the Aragonese camp, killing most of the soldiers there and carrying the army's provisions back into the town. A final charge by the cavalry of the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> broke the Christian army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Having lost most of his men, Alfonso fled towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, and he died on 7 September 1134. The losses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a> were heavy, and many notable members of Aragonese society were among the dead or captured. The French knights killed included Aimery II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, Centule VI of Béarn and Bertrán de Risnel, while Guy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lescar">Lescar</a>, who fought with the Christian forces, was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> and imprisoned at Balensiyya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Fraga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He died in 1134 soon after his defeat at the <b>Battle of Fraga</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Military</a></li></ul></section>
